    "body": "## Summary\n\n- A user's NIP-05 was not resolving despite the name-server DB showing the record as active with a pubkey assigned. Root cause: the worker's `FASTLY_STORE_ID` secret was set to the wrong store, so every `syncUsernameToFastly` call was writing to a store that divine-router never reads. No error was surfaced anywhere.\n- Fixed the secret manually (2026-04-30). This issue covers making the system resilient to this class of failure.\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. Move `FASTLY_STORE_ID` from secret to checked-in config (`wrangler.toml` vars)**\nThe store ID is a resource identifier, not a credential. Checking it in prevents silent drift and makes it reviewable.\n\n**2. Admin UI: NIP-05 resolution status on username detail page**\nAfter looking up a username, fetch the actual `.well-known/nostr.json` endpoint and show a green/red indicator for whether it resolves. Currently the admin UI shows DB state but has zero visibility into edge resolution.\n\n**3. Admin UI: \"Re-sync to Fastly\" button**\nTrigger `syncUsernameToFastly` for a single username from the detail page. Currently the only options are re-assign (side effects on timestamps/source) or running the reconcile script.\n\n**4. Sync verification in the write path**\nAfter `syncUsernameToFastly` writes a KV entry, read it back to confirm it landed. Log a warning if verification fails. This catches wrong store ID, expired token, or API failures that the current fire-and-forget `waitUntil` silently swallows.\n\n## Context\n\n- divine-router (Fastly Compute) reads from KV store `divine-names` (`gclbp6suv4bjnqpctp2b7n`)\n- divine-name-server (CF Worker) writes to whatever `FASTLY_STORE_ID` is set to\n- Hourly cron reconciliation also times out at 90K+ users\n- Self-service claims fire sync via `waitUntil` (fire-and-forget) with no feedback on failure",
